  "text": "**Review of *The Great Gatsby***  \n*By: [Your Name]*\n\nF. Scott Fitzgerald's *The Great Gatsby* is a timeless masterpiece that delves into the complexities of the American Dream during the Roaring Twenties. Set in the fictional city of West Egg, the story unfolds through the eyes of Nick Carraway, a young man who moves to Long Island to work in the bond business. His neighbor, Jay Gatsby, is a mysterious millionaire whose lavish parties and enigmatic past captivate the community.\n\nGatsby's mansion, located on East Egg, becomes the center of social intrigue, drawing in a host of characters such as Daisy Buchanan, Tom Buchanan, Jordan Baker, and the enigmatic Wolfsheim. The novel explores themes of love, wealth, and the corrupting influence of the American Dream. The recurring use of green light at the end of Daisy's dock symbolizes Gatsby's hopes and dreams, highlighting the unattainable nature of his aspirations.\n\nFitzgerald's rich, lyrical prose captures the essence of a bygone era, full of\u5962\u534e and excess, yet beneath the surface lies a profound sense of loss and disillusionment. The tragic fate of Gatsby serves as a poignant reminder of the dangers of idealism and the fragility of human connections in a world driven by materialism.\n\nIn conclusion, *The Great Gatsby* is not just a novel about a wealthy man's obsession with the past, but a profound commentary on the American Dream and the social stratification of the 1920s. It remains a literary work of immense significance, both in its storytelling and its enduring relevance.\n\n**Proper Nouns from the Book:**\n\n- *The Great Gatsby*  \n- West Egg  \n- Long Island  \n- Nick Carraway  \n- Jay Gatsby  \n- East Egg  \n- Daisy Buchanan  \n- Tom Buchanan  \n- Jordan Baker  \n- Wolfsheim  \n- Green Light  \n- American Dream  \n- 1920s\n\n**Total Proper Nouns Used in the Review: 13**",
